00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:31.430
Trains are expensive.

00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:36.050
So expensive, in fact, that on three of the
most travelled routes in three countries—New

00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:41.920
York to Washington in the US, Edinburgh to
London in the UK, and Paris to Lyon in France—they’re

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:44.270
pretty much the same price as the plane.

00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:51.559
These routes start at $49, £30, and €30
respectively on the train and $52, £13, and

00:00:51.559 --> 00:00:53.379
€53 on the plane.

00:00:53.379 --> 00:00:58.719
On a longer route like New York to Chicago,
the difference is even more pronounced: $59

00:00:58.719 --> 00:01:01.969
for the plane, $108 for the train.

00:01:01.969 --> 00:01:06.300
And that’s keeping in mind that trains are
subsidized or government run in almost every

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:10.870
country while airlines are highly profitable
commercial enterprises.

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:16.430
The planes flying between DC and New York
are $49 million dollar machines, while the

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:20.230
trains traveling the route cost no more than
$10 million total.

00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:24.540
The plane has to burn 1.7 gallons of fuel
per mile flown (3.9 liters per km) while the

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:27.220
train relies on cheap, clean electric power.

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:31.160
All this therefore begs the question, why
are trains so expensive?

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:37.100
Now, I mentioned that fares between DC and
New York start at $49, but that’s far from

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:38.790
the average price—$73.

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:42.530
Let’s look at the expenses that go into
that fare.

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:46.960
The single largest expense for Amtrak is staffing.

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:50.800
Trains require a lot of people to operate.

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:57.440
85,000 passengers journey on Amtrak daily,
but for that Amtrak employs 20,000 people

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:02.780
meaning that, daily, Amtrak requires one employee
for every four passengers.

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:07.800
On top of that, the majority of those working
for Amtrak are highly specialized, unionized

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:10.489
workers who demand high salaries.

00:02:10.489 --> 00:02:16.430
Amtrak’s financial reports tell us that
they spend $105,000 per employee, but that’s

00:02:16.430 --> 00:02:19.329
not to say that everyone at Amtrak is making
six figures.

00:02:19.329 --> 00:02:25.689
Taxes and benefits typically cost an employer
30-40% of a salary so the actual average salary

00:02:25.689 --> 00:02:29.329
for an Amtrak employee is around $75,000.

00:02:29.329 --> 00:02:34.650
These salary costs are so high that they account
for over a third of the ticket price between

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:37.239
DC and New York—$25.82 total.

00:02:37.239 --> 00:02:44.459
The cost of employees is so high for train
operators largely because trains are so slow.

00:02:44.459 --> 00:02:49.079
For a flight from DC to New York, an airline
would only have to pay employees for an hour

00:02:49.079 --> 00:02:53.760
of work while Amtrak has to pay their employees
for three and a half hours of work.

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:58.680
The difference is even more striking on long-haul
routes—Chicago to Los Angeles for example.

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:04.180
An airline would have to pay for four hours
of work, while Amtrak pays for 44 hours of

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:05.180
work.

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:10.719
In addition, trains have physical infrastructure
to maintain along the journey—the rails.

00:03:10.719 --> 00:03:15.230
Airlines also have infrastructure to pay for
on each end—the airports—but between those

00:03:15.230 --> 00:03:17.829
they just use the sky, which is free.

00:03:17.829 --> 00:03:24.870
Amtrak only owns 730 miles of the 21,000 miles
of track they use, but they still indirectly

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:29.989
pay for the employees who maintain those 20,000
miles of rented track through the fees charged

00:03:29.989 --> 00:03:32.159
by the track owners for their use.

00:03:32.159 --> 00:03:36.279
The next largest cost for train operators
is that of the trains themselves.

00:03:36.279 --> 00:03:40.829
Trains aren’t that expensive compared to
airplanes, but they still cost millions of

00:03:40.829 --> 00:03:41.829
dollars.

00:03:41.829 --> 00:03:46.279
The locomotive pulling the train from DC to
New York costs $6.5 million dollars and then

00:03:46.279 --> 00:03:50.639
each one of the passenger cars costs an additional
$400,000.

00:03:50.639 --> 00:03:56.840
With a seven car train, that works out to
$9.4 million dollars which accounts for $9.67

00:03:56.840 --> 00:03:58.489
on this particular ticket.

00:03:58.489 --> 00:04:05.510
The other part of infrastructure—rails—costs
Amtrak an additional $3.66 on this ticket.

00:04:05.510 --> 00:04:10.480
Railroad tracks are extraordinarily expensive
to build—typically more than $1 million

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:15.569
per mile—but on routes like DC to New York,
they’re just used so much that the per train

00:04:15.569 --> 00:04:18.480
or per ticket cost is negligible.

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:22.670
Amtrak is a business, so it also needs to
pay to run the business.

00:04:22.670 --> 00:04:29.420
$2.15 of this ticket goes to administrative
costs, and then another $1.31 to advertising.

00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:34.790
Moving on, trains are extremely safe compared
to cars, but you’re still more than 3 times

00:04:34.790 --> 00:04:38.700
as likely to be killed on or by a train than
a plane.

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:42.210
Trains do occasionally crash, and they also
crash into people.

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:46.440
When this happens, Amtrak often has to pay
a settlement to the victims, and the fees

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:51.510
associated with that account for $0.79 of
the DC to New York ticket.

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:56.080
That does mean that when traveling between
DC and New York, in essence, you’re paying

00:04:56.080 --> 00:04:59.980
$0.79 in order for Amtrak to kill or injure
people.

00:04:59.980 --> 00:05:05.340
Those were all the major costs to run a train,
but there’s still another $5.91 on that

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:08.740
ticket that just represents other minor costs.

00:05:08.740 --> 00:05:14.020
So the total expense for Amtrak to run that
train is $50.14.

00:05:14.020 --> 00:05:18.260
The remaining $22.86 is pure profit.

00:05:18.260 --> 00:05:23.280
You see, the train from DC to New York, the
Northeast Regional, is one of the few Amtrak

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:24.970
routes to make a profit.

00:05:24.970 --> 00:05:30.640
The demand, speed, and frequency of the train
helps it succeed financially where other routes

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:31.640
failed.

00:05:31.640 --> 00:05:36.550
Per passenger per mile, Amtrak makes eight
cents of profit on the northeast regional,

00:05:36.550 --> 00:05:41.980
the low speed train, and 29 cents per passenger
mile on the Acela Express, the high-speed

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:42.980
train.

00:05:42.980 --> 00:05:47.200
These profitable routes help pay for Amtrak’s
unprofitable routes… and there are a lot

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:48.200
of them.

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:53.170
Some routes like the Sunset Limited between
New Orleans and Los Angeles lose as many as

00:05:53.170 --> 00:05:59.570
21.7 cents per passenger per mile, and when
passengers can travel 2,000 miles on that

00:05:59.570 --> 00:06:01.830
route, that’s a lot of loss.

00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:07.490
As I mentioned, that $49 fare is not the average
ticket price to travel between DC and New

00:06:07.490 --> 00:06:08.490
York.

00:06:08.490 --> 00:06:13.080
The $49 fare is the price at which Amtrak
starts selling tickets, but as the date of

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:17.030
travel nears, the price can increase to hundreds
of dollars.

00:06:17.030 --> 00:06:21.490
That might seem like price gouging, but its
actually a way to make sure everyone can afford

00:06:21.490 --> 00:06:22.490
a ticket.

00:06:22.490 --> 00:06:25.760
That’s not to say Amtrak and other train
companies are these altruistic organizations

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:29.850
trying to bring travel to the masses—it
just makes more money.

00:06:29.850 --> 00:06:34.580
Especially with trains where it costs the
operator roughly the same to transport 5 passengers

00:06:34.580 --> 00:06:40.220
as it does to transport 500, the operator
always wants to have as many seats as possible

00:06:40.220 --> 00:06:42.630
filled, even if that means selling cheap tickets.

00:06:42.630 --> 00:06:46.730
In a perfect world for the operator, they
could ask every potential passenger what the

00:06:46.730 --> 00:06:50.010
maximum amount they’d be willing to pay
for a journey is.

00:06:50.010 --> 00:06:55.000
If they adapt the ticket price to every passengers
maximum price then they can fill each seat

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:57.890
with passengers paying the highest possible
amount.

00:06:57.890 --> 00:07:02.950
However, in practice, nobody would ever answer
the question truthfully so it would never

00:07:02.950 --> 00:07:03.950
work.

00:07:03.950 --> 00:07:08.000
Ticketing systems, however, try to ask this
question subliminally.

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:12.940
Going back to that route from Edinburgh to
London, the operator, Virgin Trains East Coast,

00:07:12.940 --> 00:07:17.570
sells three types of tickets—advance, off-peak,
and anytime.

00:07:17.570 --> 00:07:22.740
The advance tickets range anywhere from 30
pounds to 140 pounds, the off-peak fares cost

00:07:22.740 --> 00:07:28.280
137 pounds, and the anytime fares cost 148.50
pounds.

00:07:28.280 --> 00:07:32.590
For the advance fares, there are a certain
unknown number of tickets at different price

00:07:32.590 --> 00:07:35.780
levels on sale and once they’re gone, they’re
gone.

00:07:35.780 --> 00:07:40.420
For example, there might be 15 tickets at
30 pounds and once they’re sold, the price

00:07:40.420 --> 00:07:45.740
would increase to 35 pounds, then 40, 45,
and so on a so forth.

00:07:45.740 --> 00:07:49.120
That encourages those who can buy early to
buy early.

00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:50.790
Normally that means tourists.

00:07:50.790 --> 00:07:55.560
Tourists tend to plan far in advance and are
more budget conscious since they’re paying

00:07:55.560 --> 00:07:56.690
their own costs.

00:07:56.690 --> 00:08:00.760
They’re also more likely to travel down
to London on the often cheaper plane since

00:08:00.760 --> 00:08:03.840
they’re less attracted by the convenience
of the train.

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:08.600
These advance fares are only valid for the
exact route, day, and time bought which is

00:08:08.600 --> 00:08:13.840
fine for leisure travelers, but business travelers
typically want more flexibility.

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:17.540
Buying advance fares often doesn’t work
for business travelers since their plans are

00:08:17.540 --> 00:08:23.160
only made a few weeks or days in advance and,
since they don’t personally pay for their

00:08:23.160 --> 00:08:28.200
tickets, its no problem for them to pay for
the convenience of taking the train on a flexible

00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:29.200
ticket.

00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:33.130
That’s why they often pay £148.50 for an
anytime ticket.

00:08:33.130 --> 00:08:38.510
With these, you can just hop on a train whenever—it
doesn’t matter if its in 10 minute or 10

00:08:38.510 --> 00:08:39.510
days.

00:08:39.510 --> 00:08:41.240
You just step on the train and take a seat.

00:08:41.240 --> 00:08:45.330
The middle ground between those two is the
off-peak single which lets you take any train

00:08:45.330 --> 00:08:49.450
that arrives in London after 11:17 am or is
on the weekend.

00:08:49.450 --> 00:08:53.640
These fares are still geared towards business
travelers, but by restricting against the

00:08:53.640 --> 00:08:58.900
early morning trains they give a discount
to those who can avoid the busiest morning

00:08:58.900 --> 00:08:59.900
trains..

00:08:59.900 --> 00:09:02.580
For each of these fares there are equivalents
in first class—the advance first fares range

00:09:02.580 --> 00:09:03.943
between 40 and 200 pounds, the off-peak fares
are 185.50 pounds, and the anytime fares are

00:09:03.943 --> 00:09:04.943
236.50 pounds.

00:09:04.943 --> 00:09:07.860
On top of that, young, disabled, and elderly
people get up to 1/3 off their fares with

00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:08.860
a rail-card.

00:09:08.860 --> 00:09:13.360
This all means that there are essentially
12 different types of tickets for sale and

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:17.360
that one person heading to London might be
paying 20 pounds while the person sitting

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:21.880
right next to them is paying 200 but what
they’re really paying for is convenience.

00:09:21.880 --> 00:09:28.260
Now, back in the US, if Amtrak only operated
profitable lines, their route map would look

00:09:28.260 --> 00:09:32.420
like this, but the routes that don’t make
money are the ones that really matter.

00:09:32.420 --> 00:09:38.380
Amtrak serves over 500 destinations in 46
states—many of which are small towns with

00:09:38.380 --> 00:09:40.710
no other means of public transportation.

00:09:40.710 --> 00:09:45.310
While trains are normally the more expensive
means of transport, they are less expensive

00:09:45.310 --> 00:09:47.550
than planes to service small communities.

00:09:47.550 --> 00:09:53.600
The small airports in the rural parts of America
are extraordinarily expensive to operate.

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:57.700
Even if there are just two or three flights
a day, they still need a runway, terminal,

00:09:57.700 --> 00:10:02.960
security, and air traffic control while a
rural train station needs barely any infrastructure

00:10:02.960 --> 00:10:04.090
or maintenance.

00:10:04.090 --> 00:10:09.589
In fact, it’s cheaper to fly from Chicago
to London ($741) than it is to fly from Havre,

00:10:09.589 --> 00:10:14.250
Montana to Chicago ($811 May 17-22) whereas
Amtrak brings passengers from Havre to Chicago

00:10:14.250 --> 00:10:19.160
for only a few hundred dollars—much more
in budget for the average resident of Havre

00:10:19.160 --> 00:10:22.360
who makes only $22,000 per year.

00:10:22.360 --> 00:10:26.850
Of course this is a political issue, but a
part of why trains are so expensive is to

00:10:26.850 --> 00:10:32.130
allow train operators to fulfill obligations
to serve small communities who need solid

00:10:32.130 --> 00:10:33.560
transport links.

00:10:33.560 --> 00:10:38.580
Research has shown that ease of access to
transport has a stronger influence on whether

00:10:38.580 --> 00:10:42.570
someone will earn more than their parents
did than the level of crime in their area

00:10:42.570 --> 00:10:47.440
or whether they grew up in a two-parent household
and so keeping trains running through rural

00:10:47.440 --> 00:10:50.380
America is incredibly important.

00:10:50.380 --> 00:10:56.110
Next time you take the train from DC to New
York, just keep in mind that that $22.86 of

00:10:56.110 --> 00:11:00.930
profit goes to making sure that someone from
Havre can get to Chicago for less than you

00:11:00.930 --> 00:11:06.000
can fly to Europe.

